  "original_html": "<h1 id=\"primary-heading\" class=\"sr-only\">Home</h1><div class=\"flex flex-col gap-8\"><div class=\"bg-white p-4 shadow rounded-lg sticky top-8\"><div class=\"flex justify-between items-center\"><div class=\"flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-center gap-4\"><div class=\"w-12 h-12\"><img class=\"object-cover rounded-md w-full h-full\" src=\"/img/k/public/fc3bc20-Module%201%20Reflective%20Sustaining%20Literacy.png\" alt=\"Badge or credential logo image\"/></div><div class=\"flex flex-col\"><div><b>Name:</b> <a href=\"/u/debbie-ross\" class=\"underline text-blue-600\">Debbie Ross</a></div><div><b>Badge:</b> <a href=\"/badge/b69d53a3-709e-48bb-8aea-1e7a3748eb1d\" class=\"underline text-blue-600\">Everyday Sparks 2.0</a></div></div></div><div></div></div></div><section title=\"Request responses\" class=\"w-full space-y-5 bg-white p-4\"><div class=\"flex flex-col gap-2\"><div class=\"font-bold\">Which strategy from this module (Daily Schedule, Take a Line for a Walk or Interpreting Visual Texts) feels most immediately usable in your teaching context? Why?:</div><div class=\"flex flex-col gap-2\"><p>Take a Line for a Walk was the one I felt was most usuable in my ELA classes.</p></div></div><div class=\"flex flex-col gap-2\"><div class=\"font-bold\">What other strategies or approaches have you used to engage your students in routine writing over the course of this school year? What, if any, challenges have you encountered in engaging students in sustained routine writing?:</div><div>I have used the listing format that was presented in the first module in the fall. I also love the Say, Mean, Matter format for my college level literature class.</div></div></section></div>",
